# Provenance — charts/README.md § `SOURCE` is required, including when nothing was forked.
#
# ADR-010: "Their charts are a starting point, not a dependency. Where a chart is close, fork it into
# charts/ with the upstream commit recorded in a SOURCE file." Nothing was forked here, and
# `vendored: none` is an answer to "where did this come from" where a missing file is not.

vendored: none
upstream: https://github.com/goharbor/harbor-helm

# ⚠ THE OPERATOR ADR-010 CLAUSE 1 IMPLIES IS ARCHIVED, AND THAT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE IN THIS
# FILE. Checked against the GitHub API on 2026-08-18 rather than against a README:
#
#     GET /repos/goharbor/harbor-operator
#       "archived": true
#       "description": "[DEPRECATED] Kubernetes operator for Harbor service components"
#       "pushed_at": "2025-08-08T09:26:29Z"
#
# Its README opens "Due to low activity in maintanance this sub-project we are archiving it"; its last
# STABLE release is v1.3.0 (2022-07-02) and the only newer tag is v1.4.0-rc1 (2022-12-07), a release
# candidate that never shipped. Of the eighteen repositories in the goharbor organisation exactly two
# are archived and this is one of them.
#
# ⚠ THIRD SIGHTING. charts/managed/nats found nats-operator archived on 2025-04-10;
# CyberCloud.Search/vectorStores found qdrant/qdrant-operator answering 404; CyberCloud.DocumentDB
# found the FerretDB organisation holds no operator at all. ADR-010 clause 1 calls itself "the
# operator selection per managed service" and is, four rows in, a survey of SOFTWARE CHOICES that is
# only sometimes a survey of operators. The correction belongs in ADR-010 rather than in a provider,
# and it is reported rather than made here.
#
# So this chart renders Harbor's workload directly — the operator-less shape charts/managed/nats
# established — and `upstream` above points at goharbor/harbor-helm, which is what upstream actually
# maintains for Kubernetes: not archived, pushed 2026-08-03, latest release tag v1.19.2 (release NAME
# v2.15.2 — ⚠ the chart's tag and Harbor's app version are deliberately different numbers, so pin the
# TAG).
upstream-api: none — apps/v1, core/v1 and monitoring.coreos.com/v1 only

# The engine this chart runs. ADR-011's licence table lists Harbor as Apache-2.0.
engine: https://github.com/goharbor/harbor

# Reviewed against the upstream sources on this date. A bare date rather than a SHA is a weaker claim
# and is labelled as one.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS REVIEW ESTABLISHED, because the credential story is three different stories in three
# layers and reading any one of them alone gets it wrong:
#
#   * `goharbor/harbor-helm`, `values.yaml:295` — `harborAdminPassword: "Harbor12345"`, and
#     `templates/core/core-secret.yaml:23` consumes it with NO generation fallback. In the SAME FILE,
#     `secret` and `CSRF_KEY` (lines 15, 32), `JOBSERVICE_SECRET` and `REGISTRY_HTTP_SECRET` all end
#     in `| default (randAlphaNum 16)` or `(randAlphaNum 32)`. The administrator's password is the one
#     credential in that chart that is not randomised, so a default `helm install` is a registry
#     reachable at admin/Harbor12345 over a protocol every CI system already speaks. Opting out means
#     setting `existingSecretAdminPassword` (values.yaml:293-294).
#
#   * `goharbor/harbor` core — the CODE default is the EMPTY STRING, not Harbor12345.
#     `src/lib/config/metadata/metadatalist.go:70` gives `HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD` a `DefaultValue: ""`,
#     and `make/migrations/postgresql/0001_initial_schema.up.sql:59` seeds
#     `('admin', '', 'system admin', …)`. The 87 occurrences of `Harbor12345` in that repository are
#     tests, tooling and `make/harbor.yml.tmpl` — the offline installer's config template — and ZERO
#     are in Go source.
#
#   * `src/core/main.go:96-105` — the environment value is applied ONLY when `user.Salt == ""`, with
#     no non-empty guard, and the else branch logs "Admin password from config
#     (HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD) ignored: password already exists in database." So an unset variable
#     seeds the administrator with the hash of the empty string, ONCE, PERMANENTLY — and a second mint
#     later would not take effect at all, which is why
#     ContainerRegistryListCredentialsHandler reads and never mints.
#
#   * The archived operator made `harborAdminPasswordRef` REQUIRED and generated nothing:
#     `apis/goharbor.io/v1beta1/harbor_types.go:187-189` carries
#     `+kubebuilder:validation:Required` on it and the only consumer,
#     `controllers/goharbor/configuration/reconciler.go:137`, is a plain read. That is the safest of
#     the three behaviours and it belongs to the layer that is archived.
#
#   * `distribution`'s htpasswd backend accepts BCRYPT AND NOTHING ELSE, which is why
#     templates/configmap.yaml renders no `auth:` block — see conformance.yaml § owed,
#     `registry-accepts-unauthenticated-callers-from-inside-the-namespace`.
#
#   * The patch each offered minor pins to was read off `goharbor/harbor`'s own releases:
#     v2.14.4 (2026-05-11) and v2.15.2 (2026-07-02). Harbor publishes no `v2.15` tag, so a bare minor
#     as an image reference is an ImagePullBackOff per pod.
reviewed: 2026-08-18

# ⚠ THE ARCHIVED OPERATOR'S CRDs ARE NOT INSTALLED AND ARE NOT WHAT THIS CHART RENDERS, so the
# `Matches` containment argument every other family makes from `+kubebuilder:default` markers or a
# mutating webhook is UNAVAILABLE here rather than merely false. Recorded because the operator does
# carry both — 298 `+kubebuilder:default` markers across `apis/`, and a real
# `MutatingWebhookConfiguration` whose `HarborCluster` defaulter NILS OUT every non-selected variant
# (`apis/goharbor.io/v1beta1/harborcluster_webhook.go:38-105`) — so an author who reached for the
# operator later would find containment forced twice over. What forces it HERE is that five of the six
# kinds rendered are built-in, and a built-in kind is the most heavily defaulted object in Kubernetes.
upstream-webhook: not installed — this chart renders no goharbor.io object

